---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "manage",
 "name": "GoDaddy",
 "title": "How to Add and Manage DNS Records in GoDaddy",
 "description": "Add, edit, and delete DNS records in GoDaddy step by step, with the gotchas that break sites and mail, and free tools to verify every change.",
 "intro": "Managing DNS in GoDaddy is a short walk once you know where the records live and which defaults to watch. This is the complete flow, with the verification step most guides skip.",
 "panel": "GoDaddy account, My Products, Domains, next to the domain click DNS (or DNS, DNS Zones). The DNS Records table manages everything for domains using GoDaddy's nameservers.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open DNS records",
   "My Products, find the domain, click DNS. You land on the DNS Records table."
  ],
  [
   "Add the record",
   "Click Add New Record. Choose the type, set Name (use @ for the root), Value, and TTL (600 seconds is the default and fine)."
  ],
  [
   "Save",
   "Changes save per-record. Edits take effect after the old TTL expires, so drop TTL to 600 a day before any planned cutover."
  ],
  [
   "Verify",
   "Use the DNS record lookup to confirm exactly what the world now sees."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "GoDaddy aggressively offers DNS add-ons and parking during the flow; also, after a nameserver change to an external host, GoDaddy's own DNS Records page stops being authoritative even though it still shows your records.",
  "fieldMap": { "name": "Name", "value": "Value", "ttl": "Default 1 Hour", "apex": "@", "note": "The default 1 Hour TTL applies to new records; lower it to 600 seconds before a planned change, then raise it back." },
"faqs": [
  [
   "Is GoDaddy DNS free?",
   "Yes, DNS hosting for domains registered there is included; the paid items are add-ons (premium DNS, SSL), not basic records."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in GoDaddy?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find GoDaddy's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for {name} to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in GoDaddy?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in GoDaddy?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in {name}, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
